---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CONTACT_PATH } from '../consts';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="Enterprise roots. Automated execution. Who's building your infrastructure.">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-24">
    <span class="label-mono text-xs text-(--color-accent)">About</span>
    <h1 class="font-display mb-8 mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold text-(--color-ink)">
      Engineering, not guesswork.
    </h1>
    
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I'm John DeLeon, the engineer behind MakingCode. I design and build automated systems for
        businesses that run on reliability, not hype &mdash; real estate brokerages, service
        operators, and teams who need software that works the same way at 2am as it does at 2pm.
      </p>
      <p>
        I don't build superficial AI wrappers. I build deterministic, production-grade infrastructure 
        for operators who cannot afford payload drops or unexpected downtime.
      </p>

      <hr class="my-12 border-(--color-border)" />

      <h2 class="font-display mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-(--color-ink)">
        Enterprise Roots. Automated Execution.
      </h2>
      <p>
        Most automation architectures break under load because they lack defensive design. My approach 
        is built on <strong>14 years of professional software engineering experience</strong> architecting 
        high-availability backend systems, enterprise integration pipelines, and core business infrastructure.
      </p>
      <p>
        When I design an automation workflow, I apply the same rigorous engineering standards required by 
        enterprise systems:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-3 pl-5 list-disc my-6">
        <li>
          <strong>True Idempotency:</strong> Ensuring that if an API webhook fires three times due to a 
          network stutter, your CRM doesn't get flooded with duplicate entries.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Defensive Error Routing:</strong> Building asynchronous "fail-loud" loops so that if an 
          LLM returns a malformed payload, the data is quarantined and self-healed, never silently dropped.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Data Modeling Integrity:</strong> Constructing clean relational schemas behind the scenes 
          so your automated tools seamlessly feed your long-term reporting metrics.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <hr class="my-12 border-(--color-border)" />

      <h2 class="font-display mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-(--color-ink)">
        The Toolkit
      </h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 pl-5 list-disc my-6">
        <li><strong>Languages & Logic:</strong> TypeScript, Node.js, Python, Strict JSON Schema Validation</li>
        <li><strong>Automation & Flow:</strong> n8n, Custom Webhooks, Message Queues (BullMQ/Redis)</li>
        <li><strong>Data & AI Stack:</strong> PostgreSQL, Supabase, Advanced LLM Integration (Structured Outputs), Semantic Routing</li>
        <li><strong>Infrastructure:</strong> Docker, Cloudflare, Version-Controlled Git Architectures</li>
      </ul>

      <hr class="my-12 border-(--color-border)" />

      <h2 class="font-display mb-4 text-xl font-semibold text-(--color-ink)">
        Ready to remove the manual work?
      </h2>
      <p class="mb-8">
        If you are evaluating whether an automated system could remove manual friction, prevent data loss, 
        or accelerate response times in your operation, let's look at the math.
      </p>
      
      <div class="mt-8">
        <a
        href={CONTACT_PATH}
        class="label-mono bg-(--color-accent) px-6 py-3 text-xs text-(--color-surface) transition-transform hover:-translate-y-0.5"
      >
          START A STRUCTURAL SCOPING CONVERSATION
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>